0,Bontoc language - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bontoc_language,There are two degrees of stress in Bontoc: primary and secondary. Primary stress is phonemic and secondary stress is predictable. Both types are right-oriented and occur on one of the last three syllables.,"Bontok, Northern",Austronesian
1,Bontoc people - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bontoc_people,"Music is also important to Bontoc life, and is usually played during ceremonies. Songs and chants are accompanied by nose flutes (kalaleng), gongs (gangsa), bamboo mouth organ (affiliao), and Jew's harp (ab-a-fiw).","Bontok, Northern",Austronesian
2,Eastern Bontok language - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eastern_Bontok_language,"Eastern Bontok (Eastern Bontoc) is a language of the Bontok group spoken in the Philippines. The 2007 census claimed there were around 6,200 speakers.","Bontok, Northern",Austronesian
3,"Cañao – The Ritual Feast of Bontok | Sacred Tribal Ceremony ...Bontoc language - WikipediaBontok Music: Traditions and Instruments | PDF | Musical ...CORDILLERA INSTRUMENTAL MUSIC: TRADITIONAL, ETHNIC II Igorot ...Bontoc people - WikipediaBontoc, Central in Philippines people group profile | Joshua ...Selected Lyrics of Bontok’s “Antoway, Ayoweng, and Chag-Ay ...",https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=khdadKrsq8Q,"Jun 12, 2025 · 🪘 Experience Cañao — the sacred ritual feast of the Bontok people from the Cordillera highlands of Northern Luzon, Philippines. Led by the mombaki (tribal priest), the community gathers to... There are two degrees of stress in Bontoc: primary and secondary. Primary stress is phonemic and secondary stress is predictable. Both types are right-oriented and occur on one of the last three syllables. Bontok Music Explore the unique musical traditions of the Bontok people, an indigenous group from the mountainous region of the Cordillera #MomshieJhen #CordilleraMusic #CordilleraInstrumentalMusic #MusicOfCordilleraThis is a collection of Cordillera Insrumental Music from the provinces of Mount... Music is also important to Bontoc life, and is usually played during ceremonies. Songs and chants are accompanied by nose flutes (kalaleng), gongs (gangsa), bamboo mouth organ (affiliao), and Jew's harp (ab-a-fiw). Music, weaving, and oral tradition remain living arts. The gangsa (bronze gong) accompanies communal dances at festivals and ceremonies. The bamboo nose flute (kalaleng), Jew's harp, and bamboo mouth organ are played at social gatherings and life events. Jul 26, 2019 · In my quest to classify the role of Bontok tribe’s traditional music, the lyrics of the selected songs keep on communicating human performance through the role of music in Bontoc. What kind of music did Bontok perform? Initially, some kinds of Western popular music were adopted to perform for Bontok audiences, including country western songs and ballads. The Bontoks tend to favour any music that can be combined with the traditional style of Bontok music. Why is music important in the Bontok community? Music in the Bontok community results in the spontaneous response to group needs and involvement in communal activity. It promotes members in the society, both near and far together as a way of increasing the social relationship that connects them and the moral principles that are desired in their corporate life. Are Bontoks' songs derived from different genres? An interesting observation on the impetus regarding Bontoks’ songs is found in another publication by Sofia Bacwaden on her work with Rev. Daniel P. Carino, and it is shown in Figure 2. The figure shows that the collection of Antoway, Ayyoweng, and Chag-ay songs are derived from two suggested major genres. Is Bontok based on the Gospel? In my 28 March 2018 interview with Bacwaden, the widely acknowledged Bontok composer and choral arranger of the book Ayoweng mentions that some of her compositions are purely on love and relationships, but not directly adapted from the Gospel (interview by the researcher, Bontoc, Mountain Province).","Bontok, Northern",Austronesian
4,Bontok Music: Traditions and Instruments | PDF | Musical ...,https://www.scribd.com/document/947658085/Inbound-4583671041620745240,"Bontok Music Explore the unique musical traditions of the Bontok people, an indigenous group from the mountainous region of the Cordillera","Bontok, Northern",Austronesian
5,"CORDILLERA INSTRUMENTAL MUSIC: TRADITIONAL, ETHNIC II Igorot ...Bontoc people - WikipediaBontoc, Central in Philippines people group profile | Joshua ...Selected Lyrics of Bontok’s “Antoway, Ayoweng, and Chag-Ay ...",https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cN17_rxg5fw,"#MomshieJhen #CordilleraMusic #CordilleraInstrumentalMusic #MusicOfCordilleraThis is a collection of Cordillera Insrumental Music from the provinces of Mount... Music is also important to Bontoc life, and is usually played during ceremonies. Songs and chants are accompanied by nose flutes (kalaleng), gongs (gangsa), bamboo mouth organ (affiliao), and Jew's harp (ab-a-fiw). Music, weaving, and oral tradition remain living arts. The gangsa (bronze gong) accompanies communal dances at festivals and ceremonies. The bamboo nose flute (kalaleng), Jew's harp, and bamboo mouth organ are played at social gatherings and life events. Jul 26, 2019 · In my quest to classify the role of Bontok tribe’s traditional music, the lyrics of the selected songs keep on communicating human performance through the role of music in Bontoc. What kind of music did Bontok perform? Initially, some kinds of Western popular music were adopted to perform for Bontok audiences, including country western songs and ballads. The Bontoks tend to favour any music that can be combined with the traditional style of Bontok music. Why is music important in the Bontok community? Music in the Bontok community results in the spontaneous response to group needs and involvement in communal activity. It promotes members in the society, both near and far together as a way of increasing the social relationship that connects them and the moral principles that are desired in their corporate life. Are Bontoks' songs derived from different genres? An interesting observation on the impetus regarding Bontoks’ songs is found in another publication by Sofia Bacwaden on her work with Rev. Daniel P. Carino, and it is shown in Figure 2. The figure shows that the collection of Antoway, Ayyoweng, and Chag-ay songs are derived from two suggested major genres. Is Bontok based on the Gospel? In my 28 March 2018 interview with Bacwaden, the widely acknowledged Bontok composer and choral arranger of the book Ayoweng mentions that some of her compositions are purely on love and relationships, but not directly adapted from the Gospel (interview by the researcher, Bontoc, Mountain Province).","Bontok, Northern",Austronesian
